From: MCode <mcode@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] power: qcom_fg: read the MSOC shadow again and fix the two end points

The community fuel gauge reads the state of charge with the formula
DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST((raw - 1) * 98, 0xff - 2) + 1, which is the default
branch of the vendor driver (CAF fg-util.c, fg_get_msoc).  Two end points
are wrong and one read is not checked:

- the raw byte and its shadow register are read again until they agree,
  the way the vendor driver does (CAF fg-util.c, fg_get_msoc_raw, with
  MAX_READ_TRIES = 5), instead of silently keeping the smaller of the two
  bytes when a read is torn;
- raw 0 gives 0 % and raw 255 gives 100 %, which are the two rules of the
  vendor driver that depend on nothing else.

The rules of the vendor driver that are guarded by smooth_batt_flag (the
device tree property "qcom,fg-increase-100soc-time-2", absent from this
tree) or by report_full (which the vendor driver raises when the charger
reports CHARGE_DONE) are deliberately not applied: this driver has neither
state, and forcing them would report a state of charge that was never
measured.

No gauge register is written and no current is touched by this patch: it
only changes the way the state of charge is read and displayed.

Signed-off-by: MCode <mcode@localhost>
---
 drivers/power/supply/qcom_fg.c | 78 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 68 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)
---
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_fg.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_fg.c
@@ -478,6 +478,44 @@
  * BATTERY STATUS
  * ***********************/
 
+#define FG_MSOC_SHADOW_TRIES	5
+
+/**
+ * @brief qcom_fg_msoc_to_percent() - Convert the raw MSOC byte to a percentage
+ * @param raw Raw MSOC value as stored by the fuel gauge (0..255)
+ * @return int percentage of charge
+ *
+ * The raw MSOC is a monotonic counter, not a percentage.  The vendor driver
+ * (CAF fg-util.c, fg_get_msoc) applies three rules that depend on nothing
+ * else and that this driver applies too:
+ *
+ *   raw 0   -> 0 %
+ *   raw 255 -> 100 %
+ *   otherwise the raw byte is scaled over 1..99:
+ *       DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST((raw - 1) * (FULL_CAPACITY - 2),
+ *                          FULL_SOC_RAW - 2) + 1
+ *
+ * The scaling is the one this driver already used, with FULL_CAPACITY = 100
+ * and FULL_SOC_RAW = 255: the community formula is the default branch of the
+ * vendor driver.
+ *
+ * The other rules of the vendor driver are deliberately NOT applied: they
+ * are guarded by smooth_batt_flag, which comes from the device tree
+ * property "qcom,fg-increase-100soc-time-2" and is absent from this tree,
+ * or by report_full, which the vendor driver raises when the charger
+ * reports CHARGE_DONE.  This driver has neither state, so those branches
+ * would report a state of charge that was never measured.
+ */
+static int qcom_fg_msoc_to_percent(u8 raw)
+{
+	if (raw == 0)
+		return 0;
+	if (raw == 0xff)
+		return 100;
+
+	return DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST((raw - 1) * 98, 0xff - 2) + 1;
+}
+
 /**
  * @brief qcom_fg_get_capacity() - Get remaining capacity of battery
  *
@@ -489,18 +527,37 @@
 {
 	u8 cap[2];
 	int ret;
+	int tries = 0;
 
-	ret = qcom_fg_read(chip, cap, BATT_MONOTONIC_SOC, 2);
-	if (ret) {
-		dev_err(chip->dev, "Failed to read capacity: %d", ret);
-		return ret;
-	}
+	/*
+	 * 0x009 holds the raw MSOC (0..255) and 0x00a is its shadow
+	 * register, not the second byte of a 16 bit value.  A read can be
+	 * torn, so the two bytes are read again until they agree, the way
+	 * the vendor driver does (CAF fg-util.c, fg_get_msoc_raw, with
+	 * MAX_READ_TRIES = 5).
+	 */
+	do {
+		ret = qcom_fg_read(chip, cap, BATT_MONOTONIC_SOC, 2);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(chip->dev, "Failed to read capacity: %d", ret);
+			return ret;
+		}
+	} while (cap[0] != cap[1] && ++tries < FG_MSOC_SHADOW_TRIES);
 
 	if (cap[0] != cap[1]) {
+		/*
+		 * The vendor driver fails the read in that case.  It is not
+		 * proven on this PMIC that 0x00a is the mirror of 0x009, so
+		 * the smallest of the two is kept, which is what this driver
+		 * always did, and the situation is reported.
+		 */
+		dev_warn(chip->dev,
+			 "MSOC shadow mismatch after %d reads: %02x/%02x, keeping the smallest\n",
+			 FG_MSOC_SHADOW_TRIES, cap[0], cap[1]);
 		cap[0] = cap[0] < cap[1] ? cap[0] : cap[1];
 	}
 
-	*val = DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST((cap[0] - 1) * 98, 0xff - 2) + 1;
+	*val = qcom_fg_msoc_to_percent(cap[0]);
 
 	return 0;
 }
